String makePalindrome(String stringArg0) {
  if (stringArg0 == null || stringArg0.isEmpty) return '';

  int left = 0;
  int right = stringArg0.length - 1;

  while (left < right) {
    if (stringArg0[left] != stringArg0[right]) {
      // Find the next character to match from the right side
      bool foundMatch = false;
      for (int i = right; i > left; i--) {
        if (stringArg0[i] == stringArg0[left]) {
          right--;
          foundMatch = true;
          break;
        }
      }

      if (!foundMatch) {
        // No matching character found, add a duplicate of current left char on the right
        stringArg0 += stringArg0[left];
        right++;
      } else {
        // Move both pointers towards center
        left++;
        right--;
      }
    } else {
      // Characters match, move inward
      left++;
      right--;
    }
  }

  return stringArg0;
}
```

The code above implements an algorithm that transforms a given string into a palindrome by adding characters at the end. The algorithm works as follows:

1. It starts with two pointers: one at the beginning (`left`) and one at the end (`right`) of the string.
2. While `left` is less than `right`, it checks if the characters at these positions are equal.
3. If they're not equal:
   a. It searches from the `right` pointer towards the center to find a matching character to the one at `left`.
   b. If found, it moves the `right` pointer to this position and increments `left` and decrements `right`.
   c. If no match is found, it appends the character at `left` to the end of the string and increments `right` to account for the added character.
4. This process continues until the entire string becomes a palindrome.

This approach ensures that we build the shortest possible palindromic string by only appending necessary characters at the end.

